
  [1mThe fork:[0m whether a notification has to reach someone who does not have the app open. That single bit decides whether
  this is a client-side app or a server application, and it decides it at step 1, not step 3.

  - [1mIn-app only[0m (bell icon, badge count, notification list) — no server strictly required; can live in the browser with
    local persistence. Cheapest by a wide margin. But it only works for people already looking at the app, which for
    "tasks they care about" is often the wrong moment.
  - [1mIn-app + browser push[0m — reaches a closed tab, needs a service worker and (for real push) a server with VAPID keys.
    Moderate cost, real reach.
  - [1mIn-app + email[0m — reaches people who aren't in the app at all, needs a server, an email provider, and digest/batching
    logic so you don't spam on every field edit. Most reach, most infrastructure, and the one that makes notification
    [3mpreferences[0m a real feature rather than a toggle.

  Cheap to change later: adding a channel, if the notification record and event seam are designed channel-agnostically.
  Expensive to change later: going from no-server to server — that's a rewrite of persistence, auth, and deployment.

  My recommendation: [1min-app only for now, with a channel-agnostic notification record.[0m It's the smallest thing that
  delivers the actual value, and if the event seam and the notification model are right, email or push becomes an added
  delivery adapter rather than a redesign. I'd rather spend the budget on getting the interest model right — that's what
  makes notifications useful or noise — than on delivery plumbing.
